## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about constraints in practical feature-flag settings and to design a solver using propagation and backtracking rather than pure search. It also exposes how to craft a constructive counterexample when needed.

## Key Concepts

- Constraint satisfaction with implications and disjunctions
- Unit propagation, backtracking, and a decision trail
- Real-world constraints in deployment feature flags and release gates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal backtracker for boolean flags with given constraints
function sat(S){
  return (!S.S1 || S.S2) && (!S.S2 || S.S3) && (!S.S4 || !S.S1) && (S.S1 || S.S4) && (S.S2 || S.S4);
}
function satPartial(part){
  const v = (k)=> part.hasOwnProperty(k)? part[k] : null;
  if (v('S1')===true && v('S2')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===true && v('S3')===false) return false;
  if (v('S4')===true && v('S1')===true) return false;
  if (v('S1')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  return true;
}
function solve(partial={}, idx=0){
  const vars=['S1','S2','S3','S4'];
  if (!satPartial(partial)) return null;
  if (idx===vars.length) return partial;
  const name = vars[idx];
  for (const val of [false,true]){
    const next = Object.assign({}, partial, {[name]: val});
    const res = solve(next, idx+1);
    if (res) return res;
  }
  return null;
}
console.log(solve());
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to thousands of flags with mostly Horn clauses?
- What optimizations would you add for large constraint sets?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests temporal extension of 3-valued reasoning with forward/backward propagation and fixed-point thinking.

## Key Concepts
- Temporal 3-valued logic with Kleene semantics
- Implication as ¬A ∨ B across time slices
- Forward-chaining propagated to fixed point

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(states, rules){
  let changed=true;
  while(changed){
    changed=false;
    for(const r of rules){
      const a = evalExpr(r.ante, states);
      const b = evalExpr(r.cons, states);
      const out = imp(a,b); // Kleene A→B
      if(states[r.target] !== out){ states[r.target] = out; changed=true; }
    }
  }
  return states;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to scale to hundreds of time slices?
- How to detect unsatisfiability with partial assignments in this temporal setting?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft a new 3-valued operator and reason about propagation in a constraint system, a common pattern in policy engines and verification tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics
- Custom operator design (XOR3)
- Implication via ¬X ∨ Y under 3-valued logic
- Propagation and backtracking in constraint sets

## Code Example
```javascript
// 3-valued constants
const T = 'T', F = 'F', U = 'U';
function xor3(a,b){
  if(a==='U' || b==='U') return 'U';
  return a===b ? 'F' : 'T';
}
function imp(x,y){
  // Kleene: ¬x is T->F, F->T, U->U; then max with y (T>U>F)
  const not = (v)=> v==='T' ? 'F' : v==='F' ? 'T' : 'U';
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  const nx = not(x);
  return order[nx] >= order[y] ? nx : y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt XOR3 to a different ordering or to handle more than three truth values?
- How would you scale the propagation algorithm to 6–8 variables with richer constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design propagation strategies for tri-valued logic systems, specifically handling XOR operations in constraint networks with unknown values.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic with truth ordering T > U > F
- Three-valued XOR extension with unknown propagation
- Implication implemented as NOT A OR B with max semantics
- Local propagation ensuring no false constraint evaluations

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(a) { if(a === 'T') return 'F'; if(a === 'F') return 'T'; return 'U'; }
function xor(a, b) { if(a === 'U' || b === 'U') return 'U'; return a !== b ? 'T' : 'F'; }
function implies(a, b) { return not(a) === 'T' || b === 'T' ? 'T' : not(a) === 'U' || b === 'U' ? 'U' : 'F'; }
function propagate(variables) {
  // Iterative application of constraints until fixed point
  let changed;
  do {
    changed = false;
    // Apply each constraint using the three-valued operators
    const newV3 = xor(variables.V1, variables.V2);
    if(newV3 !== variables.V3) { variables.V3 = newV3; changed = true; }
    const newV5 = implies(not(variables.V2), variables.V4);
    if(newV5 !== variables.V5) { variables.V5 = newV5; changed = true; }
    // Continue with remaining constraints...
  } while(changed);
  return variables;
}
```

## How It Works
The propagation strategy iteratively applies constraints using Kleene semantics, ensuring that unknown values propagate appropriately while maintaining consistency. When a constraint involves an unknown variable, the result inherits the unknown state, preventing false evaluations while allowing partial information to flow through the network.
